or', 'final': None, 'trace': trace}\\n            observation = tool_observations.get(action, '')\\n            next_state = next_by_obs.get(observation, default_next)\\n            trace.append({\\n                'step': step_count,\\n                'state': current_state,\\n                'action': action,\\n                'observation': observation\\n            })\\n            step_count += 1\\n            if next_state not in transitions:\\n                return {'status': 'error', 'final': None, 'trace': trace}\\n            current_state = next_state\\n        else:\\n            return {'status': 'error', 'final': None, 'trace': trace}\\n    \\n    state_def = transitions.get(current_state)\\n    if state_def and state_def.get('kind') == 'final':\\n        final_value = state_def.get('value')\\n        return {'status': 'completed', 'final': final_value, 'trace': trace}\\n    else:\\n        return {'status': 'max_steps_reached', 'final': None, 'trace': trace}\\n\"\n\nNow we need to put that inside a JSON string, and the whole JSON object must be on one line? Not required, but to be safe, we can pretty-print. The output must be a valid JSON object. We'll produce it as a compact JSON to avoid any accidental newline issues. But the instruction says \"Your entire response must be exactly one valid JSON object\". It doesn't forbid whitespace, but to be safe, we'll output a single line. However, the source string will contain \\n which are valid. So the JSON string will have literal backslash and n characters.\n\nLet's construct the final JSON. I'll copy the source string with \\n.\n\nNote: The source has single quotes, so fine. The only potential issue is if the source contains a backslash, but it doesn't. Also, we need to ensure that the source string does not contain double quotes (it doesn't). So we can wrap it in double quotes.\n\nBut careful: In the JSON string, we need to escape double quotes inside the string. Since there are none, fine. Also, we mu